conclusions:
The present work further deepens the previous findings in the adaptation of CCDs as a high-performance derivative of RSDs for reliability applications. Numerically determined effects for extrapolation-favoring adjustments of the designs in the multivariate sphere are supplemented here by a further tool for prediction variance assessment – the EVG. In addition to established tools as FDS and VDG, a direction-dependent variance evaluation by EVG is presented, which allows for following findings:
By redefining the original  formula (Eq. 4), firstly the  can be evaluated more specifically and thus a qualified parameter range for a certain quality level can be determined for extrapolated predictions (range );
This can be further specified by individual spectra of the , the direction vector of extrapolation or its distance;
Based on these, any additional set of test runs, test results already performed as an addition in the design matrix or field data can be evaluated with regard to the extrapolation capability of the resulting design or model;
The visualization in the EVG allows for specific directions of view in relation to the  to be specifically assessed and compared depending on a wide range of data sets;
The representation offers a decisive advantage in design evaluation for multivariate reliability modeling.
In summary, the EVG approach thus offers two decisive improvements in RSM: evaluation of data points with regard to the extrapolation capability of a design and evaluability in the “working range” for reliability models. This will enable future test designs that correspond to the CCD to be adapted for ALT and fully analyzed in terms of prediction capability in the extrapolation range.
